Ported (Rust to Rust, restructured) into
crates/kgraf-session/src/approval_store.rs: `ToolPermissionStore`'s
overall shape -- one JSON file, loaded whole, one record per rule (tool, a
match, a timestamp, a note) -- and its atomic-write idea (a temp file, then a
rename over the target).

The temp-file-then-rename step itself is **not** a literal port of goose's
`save`: goose writes a fixed `tool_permissions.tmp` name with no `fsync` and
no lock, which loses a rule under two concurrent `allow()` calls (an owner
review found this after the first port landed). kGraf's
`write_store_file` instead uses a per-write unique temp name, `fsync`s the
temp file and (best effort) the containing directory, and every
read-modify-write in `insert` runs under an exclusive `flock` on a sidecar
`.lock` file, re-reading the store fresh before writing so two writers cannot
each read the same stale state and overwrite one another.

Changes, all deliberate:
- Goose keys a record on `tool_name:blake3(exact arguments)`, an exact-context
  cache entry. The task asks for "always allow this command pattern for this
  project" (Phase 3 build plan), which is a *pattern*, not one exact call, so
  kGraf's `AllowRule` carries a `Scope` (`AnyCall`, requiring an explicit
  `ConfirmAllowAnyCall` marker to construct, or `Prefix(String)`, matched with
  the same word-boundary rule `kgraf_session::skills::word_boundary_prefix`
  uses for `allowed-tools`' `Name(prefix:*)` form), never a content hash and
  never an implicit "match everything" default.
- The subject a `Prefix` rule is checked against is read from
  `Tool::permission_subject` -- the same call the rest of the approval chain
  uses -- inside `PersistedAllowHook`, never guessed from a raw `cmd`/`command`
  input field the way an earlier version of this file did. A tool that has not
  parsed its input as one simple command reports no subject at all, so a
  `Prefix` rule cannot match a compound command it was never meant to cover.
- One store per project (goose has one global store under its config dir),
  because approval is scoped "per project" (Phase 3 build plan); the store's
  path is refused if it lies inside the workspace root it is meant to gate,
  and the on-disk file carries an explicit schema version an unrecognized
  value is rejected against, both to keep the store outside a worker's own
  write access.
- `readable_context` is dropped: not used by anything in kGraf's approval
  chain. `note` is added instead, naming why the rule was added, because the
  task requires the store be "auditable".
- Applied through [`kgraf_tools::approval::ApprovalHook`]
  (`PersistedAllowHook`) rather than a bespoke check call, so it composes with
  the rest of the approval chain (0055) instead of being a second gate beside
  it.

